debug-lambda-in-a-loop-reinstrumented -- under a debug session a LAMBDA in a loop is woven again per iteration and never compiled
==================================================================================================================

Status: TODO
Priority: P3
Found: 2026-10-06, closing deferred-debugger-compiler-two-bodies.

Symptom
-------

lambda-in-a-loop-never-compiles (2.0.23) fixed the PLAIN fork: all the
closures built from one LAMBDA form share a usubr-site, which counts calls
and publishes the compiled body. The debug side has no such sharing:

- maybe-compile-instrumented-usubr (autolisp-runtime api.lisp) counts on the
  closure's own call-count, so a closure built per iteration never reaches
  the threshold: its debugging body stays interpreted however hot the loop;
- each new closure is woven again by the instrumenter on its first call
  (maybe-instrument-usubr), with a fresh function id and metadata record.

eval-lambda-form shares only the plain compiled body, deliberately: "an
instrumented body carries form-ids tied to one function's debug metadata,
so sharing it would make the debugger step through the wrong record".

Repro (reasoned from the code, not yet measured): under a debug session,
(repeat 10000 (mapcar '(lambda (x) (* x x)) '(1 2 3))) -- count
instrumentations and compiled-instrumented bodies.

Fix direction
-------------

Make the debug record per CODE, like the compiled body: the site holds the
instrumented body, its metadata (one function id for the lambda form) and
its compiled debugging body; closures from the site inherit them. Check
what breakpoints and the shadow stack key on (function id vs closure)
before changing it -- a breakpoint on the lambda's line should then hit
every closure, which is arguably what the user means.

Acceptance
----------

Under a session, a hot lambda in a loop is woven once and compiled once;
breakpoints and stepping inside it behave as for a DEFUN; test in
autolisp-compiler/tests/instrumented-tests.lisp.
